            "title": "RHVsbGVzIEludGVybmF0aW9uYWwgQWlycG9ydA==",
            "description": "",
            "content": "| Washington Dulles International Airport\n |\n| --- |\n| [![Image 1](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b1/Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_logo.svg/250px-Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_logo.svg.png)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_logo.svg) |\n| [![Image 2](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_at_Dusk.jpg/330px-Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_at_Dusk.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_at_Dusk.jpg)\n\nThe [Main Terminal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_Main_Terminal \"Dulles International Airport Main Terminal\") at dusk in August 2011\n\n\n\n |\n| \n\n*   [IATA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IATA_airport_code \"IATA airport code\"): IAD\n*   [ICAO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ICAO_airport_code \"ICAO airport code\"): KIAD\n*   [FAA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration \"Federal Aviation Administration\") [LID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Location_identifier#FAA_identifier \"Location identifier\"): IAD\n*   [WMO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Location_identifier#WMO_station_identifiers \"Location identifier\"): 72403\n\n\n\n |\n| Summary |\n| Airport type | Public |\n| Owner | [United States government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_government_of_the_United_States \"Federal government of the United States\") |\n| Operator | [Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority \"Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority\") |\n| Serves | [Washington, D.C. metropolitan area](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_metropolitan_area \"Washington metropolitan area\") |\n| Location | [Dulles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles,_Virginia \"Dulles, Virginia\"), [Virginia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia \"Virginia\"), U.S. |\n| Opened | November 17, 1962;  \n63 years ago |\n| [Hub](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airline_hub \"Airline hub\") for | \n\n*   [Southern Airways Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Airways_Express \"Southern Airways Express\")[\\[1\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-1)\n*   [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\")\n\n\n\n |\n| [Time zone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_zone \"Time zone\") | [EST](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Time_Zone \"Eastern Time Zone\") ([UTC−05:00](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC%E2%88%9205:00 \"UTC−05:00\")) |\n|  • Summer ([DST](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylight_saving_time \"Daylight saving time\")) | EDT ([UTC−04:00](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTC%E2%88%9204:00 \"UTC−04:00\")) |\n| Elevation [AMSL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Height_above_sea_level \"Height above sea level\") | 312 ft / 95 m |\n| [Coordinates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geographic_coordinate_system \"Geographic coordinate system\") | [38°56′40″N 077°27′21″W / 38.94444°N 77.45583°W](https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Dulles_International_Airport&params=38_56_40_N_077_27_21_W_region:US-VA_type:airport) |\n| Website | [flydulles.com](http://www.flydulles.com/) |\n| Maps |\n| [![Image 3: FAA airport diagram](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/IAD_FAA_diagram.pdf/page1-270px-IAD_FAA_diagram.pdf.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:IAD_FAA_diagram.pdf \"FAA airport diagram\")  \nFAA airport diagram |\n| ![Image 4: Map](https://maps.wikimedia.org/img/osm-intl,a,a,a,250x200.png?lang=en&domain=en.wikipedia.org&title=Dulles_International_Airport&revid=1328919619&groups=_dfca8d9bb16b59f3db53e962296014e6d0ed750e&parser=legacy)Interactive map of Washington Dulles International Airport\n\n |\n| [Runways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runway \"Runway\") |\n| \n\n| [Direction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runway#Naming \"Runway\") | Length | Surface |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| m | ft |\n| 01L/19R | 2,865 | 9,400 | [Concrete](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concrete \"Concrete\") |\n| 01C/19C | 3,505 | 11,500 | Concrete |\n| 01R/19L | 3,505 | 11,500 | Concrete |\n| 12/30 | 3,201 | 10,501 | Concrete |\n| _12R/30L_ | _3,200_ | _10,500_ | **Planned** |\n\n |\n| Statistics (2024) |\n| \n\n<table><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Aircraft operations</th><td>263,011</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total passengers</th><td>27,254,087</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total cargo (lbs.)</th><td>494,751,363</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n |\n| Source: [Federal Aviation Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration \"Federal Aviation Administration\"),[\\[2\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-FAA-2) Passenger traffic[\\[3\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-3) |\n\n**Washington Dulles International Airport** ( [_DUL\\-iss_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Pronunciation_respelling_key \"Help:Pronunciation respelling key\")) ([IATA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IATA_airport_code \"IATA airport code\"): **IAD**, [ICAO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ICAO_airport_code \"ICAO airport code\"): **KIAD**, [FAA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration \"Federal Aviation Administration\") [LID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Location_identifier \"Location identifier\"): **IAD**) – commonly known simply as **Dulles Airport** – is an [international airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_airport \"International airport\") serving the [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\")'s capital city, [Washington, D.C.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C. \"Washington, D.C.\") and [its surrounding area](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_metropolitan_area \"Washington metropolitan area\"). It is located 26 miles (42 km) west of [downtown Washington, D.C.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downtown_/(Washington,_D.C./) \"Downtown (Washington, D.C.)\"), in [Loudoun](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loudoun_County,_Virginia \"Loudoun County, Virginia\") and [Fairfax counties](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfax_County,_Virginia \"Fairfax County, Virginia\") in [Northern Virginia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Virginia \"Northern Virginia\").[\\[4\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Facts2-4)[\\[5\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-about-5)[\\[6\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-6)\n\nOpened in 1962, the airport is named after [John Foster Dulles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Foster_Dulles \"John Foster Dulles\"), an influential [secretary of state](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Secretary_of_State \"United States Secretary of State\") during the [Cold War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_War \"Cold War\") who briefly represented [New York](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_/(state/) \"New York (state)\") in the [United States Senate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Senate \"United States Senate\").[\\[7\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-dedapnv62-7)[\\[8\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-bbupided-8) Operated by the [Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority \"Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority\"), Dulles occupies 13,000 acres (20.3 sq mi; 52.6 km2);[\\[2\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-FAA-2)[\\[9\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-9) IAD ranks third in the United States in terms of land area, after [Denver International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver_International_Airport \"Denver International Airport\") and [Dallas Fort Worth International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dallas_Fort_Worth_International_Airport \"Dallas Fort Worth International Airport\"). The \"striking\" and \"iconic\" [Eero Saarinen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen \"Eero Saarinen\")\\-designed [Main Terminal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_Main_Terminal \"Dulles International Airport Main Terminal\")[\\[10\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-10) received vast acclaim for its innovative architecture when it was completed and has gone on to win numerous awards.[\\[11\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-11)[\\[12\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-12)[\\[13\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-13)\n\nDulles is the second busiest of three major airports serving the [Washington–Baltimore metropolitan area](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington%E2%80%93Baltimore_combined_statistical_area \"Washington–Baltimore combined statistical area\"), behind [Baltimore/Washington International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltimore/Washington_International_Airport \"Baltimore/Washington International Airport\") (BWI) and ahead of [Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan_Washington_National_Airport \"Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport\") (DCA).[\\[14\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-14) As of 2024, it is the [24th-busiest airport in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_the_busiest_airports_in_the_United_States \"List of the busiest airports in the United States\"),[\\[15\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-wapo112714-15) with 27.3 million enplanements.[\\[16\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:8-16) It had more than 20 million passenger enplanements every year from 2004 to 2019. An average of 60,000 passengers pass through Dulles daily to and from more than 139 destinations around the world.[\\[17\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-traffic_statistics-17)[\\[18\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-maps-18)[\\[19\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-19) Dulles has the most international passenger traffic of any airport in the [Mid-Atlantic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mid-Atlantic_/(United_States/) \"Mid-Atlantic (United States)\") outside the [New York metropolitan area](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_metropolitan_area \"New York metropolitan area\"), including approximately 90% of the international passenger traffic in the Baltimore–Washington region.[\\[20\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ACI-20)\n\nDulles is a [hub](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airline_hub \"Airline hub\") for [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\"), and it is frequently used by [Star Alliance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Alliance \"Star Alliance\") members that United has [codeshare agreements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Codeshare_agreement \"Codeshare agreement\") with. Dulles is also a hub for regional operators [Mesa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesa_Airlines \"Mesa Airlines\"), [GoJet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GoJet_Airlines \"GoJet Airlines\"), and [CommuteAir](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CommuteAir \"CommuteAir\"), which operate under the [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\") brand.\n\nBefore [World War II](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II \"World War II\"), [Hoover Field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoover_Field \"Hoover Field\") was the main commercial airport serving Washington, on the site now occupied by [the Pentagon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pentagon \"The Pentagon\") and its parking lots. It was replaced by [Washington National Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan_Washington_National_Airport \"Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport\") in 1941, a short distance southeast. After the war, in 1948, the [Civil Aeronautics Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_Aeronautics_Administration_/(United_States/) \"Civil Aeronautics Administration (United States)\") began to consider sites for a second major airport to serve the nation's capital.[\\[21\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Scheel-21) [Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/81st_United_States_Congress \"81st United States Congress\") passed the Washington Airport Act in 1950 to provide funding for a new airport in the region.[\\[22\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-22) The initial CAA proposal in 1951 called for the airport to be built in [Fairfax County](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfax_County,_Virginia \"Fairfax County, Virginia\") near what is now [Burke Lake Park](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burke_Lake_Park \"Burke Lake Park\"), but protests from residents, as well as the rapid expansion of Washington's suburbs during the time, led to reconsideration of this plan.[\\[23\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-vdh40-23) One competing plan called for the airport to be built in the Pender area of Fairfax County, while another called for the conversion of [Andrews Air Force Base](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrews_Air_Force_Base \"Andrews Air Force Base\") in [Prince George's County](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_George%27s_County,_Maryland \"Prince George's County, Maryland\"), [Maryland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryland \"Maryland\"), into a commercial airport.[\\[21\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Scheel-21)\n\nThe current site was selected by President Eisenhower in 1958;[\\[23\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-vdh40-23) the Dulles name was chosen by Eisenhower's aviation advisor [Pete Quesada](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pete_Quesada \"Pete Quesada\"), who later served as the first head of the [Federal Aviation Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration \"Federal Aviation Administration\"). As a result of the site selection, the unincorporated, largely African-American community of [Willard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willard,_Virginia \"Willard, Virginia\"), which once stood in the airport's current footprint, was demolished, and 87 property owners had their holdings condemned.[\\[21\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Scheel-21)\n\nDulles was also built over a lesser-known airport named Blue Ridge Airport, chartered in 1938 by the U.S. The airport was Loudoun County's first official airport, consisting of two grass intersecting runways in the shape of an \"X\". The location of the former Blue Ridge Airport sits where the Dulles Air Freight complex and Washington Dulles Airport Marriott now sit today.[\\[24\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-24)\\[_[better source needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NOTRS \"Wikipedia:NOTRS\")_\\]\n\n### Design and construction\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Design and construction\")\\]\n\n[![Image 5](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Dulles_International_Airport_%281970%29.jpg/250px-Dulles_International_Airport_%281970%29.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dulles_International_Airport_/(1970/).jpg)\n\nDulles Airport in April 1970, showing the main terminal's original size\n\nThe civil engineering firm [Ammann and Whitney](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammann_and_Whitney \"Ammann and Whitney\") was named lead contractor. The airport was dedicated by President [John F. Kennedy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy \"John F. Kennedy\") and Eisenhower on November 17, 1962.[\\[7\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-dedapnv62-7)[\\[8\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-bbupided-8)[\\[25\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-25) As originally opened, the airport had three long runways (current day runways 1C/19C, 1R/19L, and 12/30) and one shorter one (where current taxiway Q is located). Its original name, Dulles International Airport, was changed in 1984 to Washington Dulles International Airport.[\\[26\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-26)\n\nThe [main terminal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_Main_Terminal \"Dulles International Airport Main Terminal\") was designed in 1958 by famed Finnish-American architect [Eero Saarinen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen \"Eero Saarinen\"), and it is highly regarded for its graceful beauty, suggestive of flight. The terminal was built without any concourses and gates as all aircraft were parked at remote sites. Passengers were bussed to their aircraft by way of mobile lounges that raised up to the aircraft level; some are still in use today. The first midfield terminal that included gates and jetbridges was constructed in 1985 when [New York Air](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Air \"New York Air\") and other airlines began hub operations at Dulles.\\[_[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")_\\] In the 1990s, the main terminal at Dulles was reconfigured to allow more space between the front of the building and the ticket counters. Additions at both ends of the main terminal more than doubled the structure's length. The original terminal at [Taiwan Taoyuan International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_Taoyuan_International_Airport \"Taiwan Taoyuan International Airport\") in Taoyuan, Taiwan, was modeled after the Saarinen terminal at Dulles.[\\[27\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-archdaily-27)[\\[28\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-28)\n\nThe design included a landscaped man-made lake to collect rainwater, a low-rise hotel, and a row of office buildings along the north side of the main parking lot. The design also included a two-level road in front of the terminal to separate arrival and departure traffic and a federally owned [limited access highway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_Access_Road \"Dulles Access Road\") connecting the terminal to the Capital Beltway ([I-495](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstate_495_/(Capital_Beltway/) \"Interstate 495 (Capital Beltway)\")) about 17 miles (27 km) to the east; the highway system eventually grew to include a parallel toll road to handle commuter traffic and an extension to connect to [I-66](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-66 \"I-66\"). The access road had a wide median strip to allow the construction of a passenger rail line, which opened as [an extension of the Washington Metro's Silver Line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Line_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Silver Line (Washington Metro)\") on November 15, 2022.[\\[29\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:1-29)\n\nBy 1985 the original design, featuring [mobile lounges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_lounge \"Mobile lounge\") to meet each plane, was no longer well-suited to Dulles's role as a hub airport. Instead, midfield concourses were constructed to allow passengers to walk between connecting flights without visiting the main terminal. Mobile lounges were still used for international flights and to transport passengers between the midfield concourses and the main terminal; Concourse C/D was the first to be built, followed by Concourse A/B. A tunnel (consisting of a passenger walkway and moving sidewalks) that links the main terminal and Concourse B was opened in 2004.[\\[30\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-walkway-30) The [Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority \"Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority\") (MWAA) began a renovation program for the airport including a new security mezzanine with more room for lines.[\\[31\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Improvement-31)\n\nA new train system, dubbed [AeroTrain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AeroTrain_/(Washington_Dulles_International_Airport/) \"AeroTrain (Washington Dulles International Airport)\") and developed by [Mitsubishi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitsubishi_Heavy_Industries \"Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\"), began in 2010 to transport passengers between the concourses and the main terminal.[\\[32\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-aerotrain-32) The system, which uses rubber tires and travels along a fixed underground guideway,[\\[32\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-aerotrain-32) is similar to the people mover systems at [Singapore Changi Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singapore_Changi_Airport \"Singapore Changi Airport\"),[\\[32\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-aerotrain-32) [Hartsfield–Jackson Atlanta International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hartsfield%E2%80%93Jackson_Atlanta_International_Airport \"Hartsfield–Jackson Atlanta International Airport\"), and [Denver International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver_International_Airport \"Denver International Airport\"). The train is intended to replace the mobile lounges, which many passengers found crowded and inconvenient. The initial phase includes the main terminal station, a permanent Concourse A station, a permanent Concourse B station, a permanent midfield concourse station (with access to the current temporary C concourse via a tunnel with moving walkways), and a maintenance facility.[\\[32\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-aerotrain-32) Mobile lounges continue to serve Concourse D from both the main terminal and Concourse A. Even after AeroTrain is built out and the replacement Concourses C and D are built, the mobile lounges and plane mates will still continue to be used, to transport international arriving passengers to the International Arrivals Building, as well as transport passengers to aircraft parked on hardstands without direct access to jet bridges. Dulles has stated that the wait time for a train does not exceed four minutes, compared to the average 15-minute wait and travel time for mobile lounges.\\[_[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")_\\]\n\nUnder the development plan, future phases would see the addition of several new midfield concourses and a new south terminal.[\\[33\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-33) A fourth runway (parallel to the existing runways 1 and 19 L&R) opened in 2008,[\\[34\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-4thRunway-34) and development plans include a fifth runway to parallel the existing runway 12–30.[\\[35\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-5thRunway-35) If this runway is built, the current runway will be re-designated as 12L-30R while the new runway will be designated 12R-30L. An expansion of the B concourse, used by many low-cost airlines as well as international arrivals, has been completed, and the building housing Concourses C and D will eventually be knocked down to make room for a more ergonomic building. Because Concourses C and D are temporary concourses, the only way to get to those concourses is via moving walkway from the Concourse C station, which is built in the location of the future gates and Concourse D by mobile lounge from the main terminal.[\\[36\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-WUSA-36)[\\[37\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-D2_Project_AeroTrain-37)\n\n[![Image 6](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Escalators_near_departure_zones_3_and_4_at_Dulles_International_Airport.jpg/250px-Escalators_near_departure_zones_3_and_4_at_Dulles_International_Airport.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Escalators_near_departure_zones_3_and_4_at_Dulles_International_Airport.jpg)\n\nThe interior of the main terminal, showing the escalators leading to baggage claim and arrivals\n\nIn the short term, [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\") has constructed a 20,000 square foot (1,900 m2) buildout on Concourse C between gate C18 and the AeroTrain entrance for use as a Polaris Lounge for international passengers.[\\[38\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-38) Further expansion plans include a new three-story 550,000 square foot (51,000 m2) south concourse building above the AeroTrain station for Concourse C,[\\[37\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-D2_Project_AeroTrain-37) to replace Concourse A regional gates built in 1999.[\\[39\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-39)\n\nDecades-old rules set by Congress that limit the number of takeoffs and landings, as well as distance of routes, at Reagan Airport were intended in part to keep more flights at Dulles. Those rules have been weakened by Congress over the years, however, causing Dulles to lose 200,000 passengers to Reagan between 2011 and 2013.[\\[15\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-wapo112714-15)\n\nIn 2023, construction started on a 100 MW solar power facility, battery and bus charging equipment.[\\[40\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-40) It would include the largest airport-based solar and battery development in the U.S. as part of an agreement with Dominion Energy. The solar panels would cover more than 835 acres (338 ha) on land, equivalent to the consumption of more than 37,000 Northern Virginia homes during peak production.[\\[41\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-41)\n\n### Operations and milestones\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Operations and milestones\")\\]\n\n[![Image 7](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Pat_Nixon_christens_Boeing_747_2749-18.jpg/250px-Pat_Nixon_christens_Boeing_747_2749-18.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Pat_Nixon_christens_Boeing_747_2749-18.jpg)\n\nFirst Lady [Pat Nixon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pat_Nixon \"Pat Nixon\") christening the first [Boeing 747](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747 \"Boeing 747\") at Dulles on January 15, 1970.\n\nThe first scheduled flight at Dulles was an [Eastern Air Lines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Air_Lines \"Eastern Air Lines\") [Lockheed L-188 Super Electra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lockheed_L-188_Electra \"Lockheed L-188 Electra\") from [Newark International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newark_International_Airport \"Newark International Airport\") in [New Jersey](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Jersey \"New Jersey\") on November 19, 1962.[\\[42\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Facts-42) The first scheduled transatlantic nonstop flight to serve the airport commenced just shy of two years later, in June 1964.[\\[43\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-43)\n\nDulles was initially considered a [white elephant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_elephant \"White elephant\"), being far out of town with few flights;[\\[44\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-daifk-44) in 1965, Dulles averaged 89 airline operations a day while [National Airport (now Reagan)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan_Washington_National_Airport \"Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport\") averaged 600 despite not allowing jets.[\\[44\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-daifk-44) Airport operations grew along with Virginia suburbs and the [Dulles Technology Corridor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_Technology_Corridor \"Dulles Technology Corridor\"); perimeter and slot restrictions at National forced long-distance flights to use Dulles. In 1969, Dulles had 2.01 million passengers while National had 9.9 million.[\\[45\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-45)\n\nThe era of [wide-body jets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wide-body_aircraft \"Wide-body aircraft\") began on January 15, 1970, when First Lady [Pat Nixon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pat_Nixon \"Pat Nixon\") christened a [Pan Am](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_American_World_Airways \"Pan American World Airways\") [Boeing 747-100](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747-100 \"Boeing 747-100\") at Dulles in the presence of Pan Am chairman [Najeeb Halaby](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Najeeb_Halaby \"Najeeb Halaby\").[\\[46\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ergpwcgj-46) Rather than a traditional champagne bottle, red, white, and blue water was sprayed on the aircraft.[\\[47\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-pchpln-47) Pan Am's first Boeing 747 flight was from New York–JFK to [London Heathrow Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Heathrow_Airport \"London Heathrow Airport\").[\\[47\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-pchpln-47)\n\nOn December 26, 1973, President Richard Nixon flew from Dulles to Los Angeles on board a United Airlines McDonnell Douglas DC-10 commercial flight instead of on Air Force One. This was due to a nationwide fuel shortage caused at the time by the [Arab oil embargo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1973_oil_crisis \"1973 oil crisis\").[\\[48\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-48) Less than two years later, on May 24, 1976, supersonic flights between the U.S. and Europe began with the arrival of a [British Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Airways \"British Airways\") [Concorde](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concorde \"Concorde\") from London–Heathrow and an [Air France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_France \"Air France\") Concorde from [Paris–Charles De Gaulle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_Gaulle_Airport \"Charles de Gaulle Airport\"); both planes were lined nose-to-nose at Dulles for photos.[\\[49\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ppupi-49)[\\[50\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-conlands-50)[\\[51\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-51)\n\nOn June 12, 1983, the [Space Shuttle _Enterprise_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Enterprise \"Space Shuttle Enterprise\") arrived at Dulles atop a [modified Boeing 747](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuttle_Carrier_Aircraft \"Shuttle Carrier Aircraft\") after touring Europe and before returning to [Edwards Air Force Base](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwards_Air_Force_Base \"Edwards Air Force Base\"). Two years later _Enterprise_ returned and was placed in a storage hangar near Runway 12/30 to await construction of a planned [expansion to the National Air and Space Museum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_F._Udvar-Hazy_Center \"Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center\"). _Enterprise_ left Dulles on April 27, 2012, for its new home at the [Intrepid Sea, Air & Space Museum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intrepid_Sea,_Air_%26_Space_Museum \"Intrepid Sea, Air & Space Museum\") in New York City.[\\[52\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-intrepid-52)\n\nOn June 1, 1985, [New York Air](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Air \"New York Air\") began a small hub operation at Dulles, with 35 daily flights to eight cities in Florida and the northeastern United States.[\\[53\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:2-53) [Colgan Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colgan_Airways \"Colgan Airways\") became a feeder carrier for New York Air with additional service to smaller cities known as New York Air Connection. In 1987, the airline merged into [Continental Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_Airlines \"Continental Airlines\"), which maintained the hub operation until 1989.[\\[53\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:2-53) On October 10, 1985, [Presidential Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_Airways_/(scheduled/) \"Presidential Airways (scheduled)\") opened its hub at the airport, and it soon began a series of code-shares – first with [Pan Am](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_Am \"Pan Am\") from mid-1986 through early 1988, then as [Continental Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_Express \"Continental Express\") on behalf of [Continental Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continental_Airlines \"Continental Airlines\") between mid-1987 and mid-1988, and finally as [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\"), on behalf of [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\"), from mid 1988 until Presidential ceased operations on December 5, 1989.[\\[54\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-54)\n\n#### United hub years, 1986 – present\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: United hub years, 1986 – present\")\\]\n\nOn May 1, 1986, [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\") began service on 16 new domestic routes creating a hub status at Dulles. Many more domestic routes and new overseas routes would later be added. [Air Wisconsin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Wisconsin \"Air Wisconsin\") and Presidential Airways soon became feeder carriers for United operating as [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\").[\\[55\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-55)\n\nIn 1990, a [United States Senate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Senate \"United States Senate\") joint resolution to change Dulles International Airport's name to **Washington Eisenhower International Airport** was proposed by [Senator Bob Dole](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Dole \"Bob Dole\"), but the bill did not pass.[\\[56\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-eisenhower-56)\n\nWhen the [SR-71](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SR-71 \"SR-71\") was retired by the military in 1990, one was flown from its birthplace at United States Air Force [Plant 42](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant_42 \"Plant 42\") in [Palmdale, California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palmdale,_California \"Palmdale, California\"), to Dulles, setting a coast-to-coast speed record at an average 2,124 mph (3,418 km/h); the trip took 64 minutes.[\\[57\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-blackbird-57) The aircraft was placed in a storage building, and is now displayed at the Smithsonian's adjacent [Udvar-Hazy Air and Space Museum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_F._Udvar-Hazy_Center \"Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center\").[\\[57\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-blackbird-57)\n\nIn 1995, the first flight of the [Boeing 777-200](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_777-200 \"Boeing 777-200\") in commercial service landed at Dulles; the flight was operated by [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\") on its transatlantic London Heathrow – Washington Dulles route.[\\[58\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-UAhistory-58)\n\nThe 2004 launch of [low-cost carrier](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low-cost_carrier \"Low-cost carrier\") [Independence Air](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independence_Air \"Independence Air\") propelled IAD from being the 24th-busiest airport in the United States to fourth, and one of the top 30 [busiest in the world](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World%27s_busiest_airports \"World's busiest airports\"). Independence Air ceased operations in January 2006, and its space in Concourse A was taken five months later by [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\").[\\[59\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-move-59) Also taking place in 2006 was the introduction of service by [Southwest Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southwest_Airlines \"Southwest Airlines\") at IAD.[\\[60\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:3-60)\n\nSignificant growth required the airport to halt the operations of its original control tower in 2007 for a taller control tower located away from the main terminal. The original tower still exists, though it is no longer used to control the airport's traffic.[\\[60\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:3-60) That year, 24.7 million passengers passed through the airport.[\\[60\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:3-60)\n\n[![Image 8: Dulles' old air traffic control tower, which halted operations in 2007](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Dulles_Airport_tower_2008.jpg/250px-Dulles_Airport_tower_2008.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dulles_Airport_tower_2008.jpg)\n\nDulles' old air traffic control tower, which halted operations in 2007\n\n[![Image 9: The current air traffic control tower dwarfs the original one](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Jonescrusher.JPG/250px-Jonescrusher.JPG)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Jonescrusher.JPG)\n\nThe current air traffic control tower dwarfs the original one\n\nOn November 20, 2008, a third parallel north–south runway – Runway 1L/19R – opened on the west side of the airfield; the original 1L/19R was re-designated 1C/19C. It was the first new runway to be built at Dulles since the airport's construction.[\\[42\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Facts-42)\n\nOn June 6, 2011, the airport received its first [Airbus A380](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A380 \"Airbus A380\") flights when [Air France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_France \"Air France\") introduced the A380 on its nonstop from Paris [Charles de Gaulle Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_Gaulle_Airport \"Charles de Gaulle Airport\") during peak season.[\\[42\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Facts-42)\n\nOn April 17, 2012, the [Space Shuttle _Discovery_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Discovery \"Space Shuttle Discovery\") was ferried to Dulles mounted to a [NASA 747-100](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuttle_Carrier_Aircraft \"Shuttle Carrier Aircraft\") as part of its decommissioning and installation in the [Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_F._Udvar-Hazy_Center \"Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center\").[\\[61\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-61) A few weeks later, on June 1, the first passenger flight of the [Boeing 747-8 Intercontinental](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747-8_Intercontinental \"Boeing 747-8 Intercontinental\") – operated by Lufthansa with service from [Frankfurt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankfurt_Airport \"Frankfurt Airport\") to Washington – landed at IAD.[\\[62\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-62) Two months later, on August 15, [Ethiopian Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopian_Airlines \"Ethiopian Airlines\") began operating service to Dulles using the [Boeing 787 Dreamliner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_787_Dreamliner \"Boeing 787 Dreamliner\").[\\[63\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-63)\n\nIn the early 2010s, increased domestic travel from Reagan National Airport eroded some of Dulles's domestic routes.[\\[15\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-wapo112714-15) Dulles overtook Reagan in total enplanements in 2019.[\\[64\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-64)\n\nOn October 2, 2014, [British Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Airways \"British Airways\") began using the [Airbus A380](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A380 \"Airbus A380\") on flights from London–Heathrow to Dulles.[\\[65\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-65) Less than two years later, on February 1, 2016, [Emirates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirates_/(airline/) \"Emirates (airline)\") upgraded its direct flights from [Dubai International](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubai_International \"Dubai International\") – a service previously operated using a [Boeing 777](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_777 \"Boeing 777\") – to an [Airbus A380](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A380 \"Airbus A380\").[\\[66\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-66) British Airways temporarily ended A380 flights in the latter half of the decade, reverting to a [747-400](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/747-400 \"747-400\") twice daily during peak season but resumed its once-daily A380 operations during non-peak season in October 2019, before once again ending A380 service to Dulles in early 2020.\\[_[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")_\\] By 2019, Washington Dulles was one of just fourteen airports in the United States seeing daily operations from and/or having at least one gate and one runway that can accommodate an [Airbus A380](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A380 \"Airbus A380\"); the others being [Atlanta](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hartsfield-Jackson_International_Airport \"Hartsfield-Jackson International Airport\"), [Boston](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logan_International_Airport \"Logan International Airport\"), [Chicago-O'Hare](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_O%27Hare_International_Airport \"Chicago O'Hare International Airport\"), [Dallas/Fort Worth](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dallas_Fort_Worth_International_Airport \"Dallas Fort Worth International Airport\"), [Denver](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver_International_Airport \"Denver International Airport\"), [Honolulu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honolulu_International_Airport \"Honolulu International Airport\"), [Houston-Intercontinental](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houston_Intercontinental_Airport \"Houston Intercontinental Airport\"), [Las Vegas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McCarran_International_Airport \"McCarran International Airport\"), [Los Angeles–LAX](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_International_Airport \"Los Angeles International Airport\"), [Miami](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami_International_Airport \"Miami International Airport\"), [New York–JFK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy_International_Airport \"John F. Kennedy International Airport\"), [Orlando](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orlando_International_Airport \"Orlando International Airport\") and [San Francisco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Francisco_International_Airport \"San Francisco International Airport\").[\\[67\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-67)\n\nOn May 16, 2018, [Volaris Costa Rica](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volaris_Costa_Rica \"Volaris Costa Rica\") launched flights to Dulles, becoming the first international [low-cost carrier](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low-cost_carrier \"Low-cost carrier\") to serve the airport.[\\[68\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-68) A few months later, on September 15, [Cathay Pacific](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathay_Pacific \"Cathay Pacific\") launched its longest nonstop route connecting Dulles to [Hong Kong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hong_Kong_International_Airport \"Hong Kong International Airport\") utilizing an [Airbus A350-1000](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A350-1000 \"Airbus A350-1000\"); the service was suspended indefinitely due to the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\").[\\[69\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-69)[\\[70\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-70)\n\nIn 2019, four new major international routes were added. [Alitalia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alitalia \"Alitalia\") began non-stop service utilizing an [Airbus A330](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A330 \"Airbus A330\") to [Rome-Fiumicino](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonardo_da_Vinci-Fiumicino_Airport \"Leonardo da Vinci-Fiumicino Airport\"), operating five times weekly during the peak summer season, reducing to three times weekly during the winter season.[\\[71\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-71) [EgyptAir](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EgyptAir \"EgyptAir\") operates a Boeing 787–9 with nonstop service to [Cairo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cairo_International_Airport \"Cairo International Airport\") three times a week year-round.[\\[72\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-72) [TAP Air Portugal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TAP_Air_Portugal \"TAP Air Portugal\") flies five times weekly with nonstop service to [Lisbon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon_Airport \"Lisbon Airport\") on board the Airbus A321LR, A330-900 and sometimes the A330-200. By that May, United began non-stop service to [Tel-Aviv](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Gurion_Airport \"Ben Gurion Airport\"), initially utilizing a Boeing 777-200ER on a thrice-weekly schedule.\n\nIn 2020, LOT Polish, Iberia and Swiss were all scheduled to begin service to Dulles, but these were postponed due to the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\"). LOT Polish were scheduled to provide [Boeing 787](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_787 \"Boeing 787\") service from Warsaw, Iberia to provide [Airbus A330-300](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A330-300 \"Airbus A330-300\") service from Madrid, and Swiss to provide Airbus A330-300 service from Zürich. So far only the Iberia and the Swiss routes have been implemented.\\[_[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")_\\]\n\nIn 2021, regional airline [Southern Airways Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Airways_Express \"Southern Airways Express\") moved their East Coast hub from BWI to Dulles.[\\[73\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:4-73) The airline announced it would operate flights between Dulles and small airports in Pennsylvania and West Virginia, some of them on [Essential Air Service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essential_Air_Service \"Essential Air Service\") contracts.[\\[73\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:4-73)\n\nOn November 15, 2022, the airport's [Washington Metro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Metro \"Washington Metro\") [station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_station \"Dulles International Airport station\") opened as part of the Phase 2 extension of the [Silver Line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Line_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Silver Line (Washington Metro)\"), from [Wiehle–Reston East station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiehle%E2%80%93Reston_East_station \"Wiehle–Reston East station\") to [Ashburn](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashburn_station_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Ashburn station (Washington Metro)\").[\\[29\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:1-29)\n\nIn 2024, IAD was ranked the 11th best airport in the world by [AirHelp](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AirHelp \"AirHelp\"), a passenger rights tech company assisting passengers with flight disruptions. Data was compiled for 239 of the world's busiest airports in 69 countries. Data was compiled from May 1, 2023, to April 30, 2024. Criteria was based on on-time performance, customer opinion, and food and shops. Dulles' ranking was an overall 8.22 out of 10.[\\[74\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-74)\n\nBeginning in 2024, an effort by Congress has been made to rename the airport as the Donald J. Trump International Airport, in honor of President [Donald J. Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump \"Donald Trump\").[\\[75\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:0-75)[\\[76\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:5-76) In April 2024, a bill was proposed in the [United States House of Representatives](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_House_of_Representatives \"United States House of Representatives\") to rename the airport after Trump, but did not pass.[\\[77\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-77)[\\[78\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-78) A new bill was introduced in the House of Representatives in January 2025, renewing the proposal to rename Dulles after Trump; this bill was announced a few days after a similar state bill was proposed in Tennessee, to rename [Nashville International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nashville_International_Airport \"Nashville International Airport\") after Trump.[\\[75\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:0-75)[\\[76\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:5-76)[\\[79\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-79)[\\[80\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-80)\n\nAs of February 2025, more than 50 airlines serve the airport.[\\[81\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-81) The majority of the market share is United and United Express while other airlines focus on international routes or domestic routes not served by United. [Airbus A380](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superjumbo \"Superjumbo\") service to Dulles during the current summer season puts the airport in 3rd place for A380 flights in the US, after [New York–JFK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy_International_Airport \"John F. Kennedy International Airport\") and [Los Angeles–LAX](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles_International_Airport \"Los Angeles International Airport\").[\\[82\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-82) [British Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Airways \"British Airways\") and [Lufthansa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufthansa \"Lufthansa\") fly the superjumbo to Dulles on a daily schedule from May to October, while [Emirates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirates_/(airline/) \"Emirates (airline)\") flies the A380 year round. Additionally, Dulles is looking to grow its international destinations in response to the regained demand for Southeast Asian service after the pandemic caused numbers to falter significantly. This growth would target cities like [Mumbai](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mumbai \"Mumbai\"), [Taipei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taipei \"Taipei\") and [Hong Kong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hong_Kong \"Hong Kong\").[\\[83\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-83)\n\nOn March 11, 2025, [Air China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_China \"Air China\") began serving IAD with the [Boeing 747-8i](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747 \"Boeing 747\") from [Beijing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beijing_Capital_Airport \"Beijing Capital Airport\").[\\[84\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-84) Air China up until then served the airport twice weekly with the [Boeing 777-300ER](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_777 \"Boeing 777\"). This added a second Air China 747 flight to the US after [JFK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JFK_Airport \"JFK Airport\"), which had been the only airport in the United States to receive Air China 747 service.[\\[85\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-85)\n\nDulles originally used **DIA** – the initials of Dulles International Airport – as its airport code.[\\[86\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:6-86) When handwritten, DIA was often misread as DCA – the code for Washington National Airport. This prompted officials to change Dulles Airport's code to **IAD** – standing for \"International Airport Dulles.\"[\\[86\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:6-86)\n\nThe airport's terminal complex consists of the **[Main Terminal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_Main_Terminal \"Dulles International Airport Main Terminal\")** (which includes four aircraft gates, known as the **\"Z\" Gates**), and two parallel midfield terminal buildings: **Concourses A/B** and **Concourses C/D**. The entire terminal complex has a total of 139 [gates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gate_/(airport/) \"Gate (airport)\") – 123 of which have [jetways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetways \"Jetways\") and 16 of which are hardstand locations[\\[87\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-iad_facts-87) from which passengers can board or disembark using the airport's plane mate vehicles.[\\[42\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Facts-42)\n\n### Inter-terminal transportation\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=12 \"Edit section: Inter-terminal transportation\")\\]\n\n[![Image 10](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Dulles_Aerotrain_terminal_B.jpg/250px-Dulles_Aerotrain_terminal_B.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dulles_Aerotrain_terminal_B.jpg)\n\nMain Terminal [AeroTrain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AeroTrain_/(Washington_Dulles_International_Airport/) \"AeroTrain (Washington Dulles International Airport)\") station\n\nConceived in early planning sessions in 1959, Dulles is one of a few remaining airports to utilize [mobile lounges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_lounge \"Mobile lounge\") (also known as \"plane mates\" or \"people movers\"), now only used for transport to the International Arrivals Building as well as transport for Concourse D. They have all been given names based on the postal abbreviations of 50 states, e.g., VA, MD, AK.[\\[88\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-88)\n\nThe [Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority \"Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority\") has partially phased out the mobile lounge system for inter-terminal passenger movements with the [AeroTrain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AeroTrain_/(Washington_Dulles_International_Airport/) \"AeroTrain (Washington Dulles International Airport)\"), an underground [people mover](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People_mover \"People mover\") that currently operates to all of the concourses except concourse D, with a passenger tunnel remaining to concourses A and B. However, the Aerotrain to concourse C stops at a terminal north of the actual concourse, leaving a significant walk from the terminal to concourse after disembarking. Plane mates remain in use to disembark international passengers and carry them to the International Arrivals Building, as well as to transport passengers to and from aircraft on the hard stands that are called H gates (i.e., those parked remotely on the apron without access to jet bridges).[\\[89\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-89)[\\[90\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-90)\n\n[![Image 11](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/2022-03-24_13_47_19_UTC_minus_4_Ticketing_and_check-in_section_within_the_main_terminal_at_Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_in_the_Dulles_section_of_Sterling%2C_Loudoun_County%2C_Virginia.jpg/250px-thumbnail.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2022-03-24_13_47_19_UTC_minus_4_Ticketing_and_check-in_section_within_the_main_terminal_at_Washington_Dulles_International_Airport_in_the_Dulles_section_of_Sterling,_Loudoun_County,_Virginia.jpg)\n\nThe Main Terminal's ceiling is suspended in a catenary curve above the check-in area\n\nDulles's main terminal houses ticketing on the upper level, baggage claim and [U.S. Customs and Border Protection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Customs_and_Border_Protection \"U.S. Customs and Border Protection\") on the lower level, and annexes for the International Arrivals Building for international passenger processing, as well as the four Z gates (used by Air Canada and United Express), H gates, various information kiosks and other support facilities. The main terminal was recognized by the [American Institute of Architects](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Institute_of_Architects \"American Institute of Architects\") in 1966 for its design concept;[\\[91\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-91) its roof is a [suspended](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suspended_structure \"Suspended structure\") [catenary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catenary \"Catenary\") providing a wide enclosed area unimpeded by any columns.[\\[92\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Giles_1960-92)[\\[93\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Public_Opinion_1961-93) Its main terminal was designed by [Eero Saarinen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen \"Eero Saarinen\"), who also designed the [TWA Flight Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TWA_Flight_Center \"TWA Flight Center\") at [John F. Kennedy International Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_F._Kennedy_International_Airport \"John F. Kennedy International Airport\").[\\[94\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-94)\n\nThe main terminal was extended in 1996 to 1,240 feet (380 m)—Saarinen's original design length—which was slightly more than double its originally constructed length of 600 feet (180 m).[\\[87\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-iad_facts-87) On September 22, 2009, an expansion to include the 41,400 square feet (3,850 m2) International Arrivals Building opened for customs and immigration processing with a capacity to process 2,400 passengers per hour.[\\[95\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-wp-95)\n\nAlso in September 2009, a 121,700 square feet (11,310 m2) central [security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transportation_Security_Administration \"Transportation Security Administration\") checkpoint was added on a new security mezzanine level of the main terminal. This checkpoint replaced previous checkpoints that were located behind the ticketing areas.[\\[96\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-96) Travelers enrolled in [TSA PreCheck](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TSA_PreCheck \"TSA PreCheck\") and [CLEAR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly_Clear \"Fly Clear\") still use this area to clear security.[\\[97\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-97) A separate security checkpoint is available on the baggage claim level for staff only, and previously had access for all passengers as the Dulles Diamond area. Both public security checkpoints connect to escalators to the AeroTrain, which links the main terminal with the A and B concourse and links to a tunnel connecting to the C concourse.\n\nAll airlines aside from certain express flights operate out of two linear [satellite terminals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_terminal \"Satellite terminal\"). Each terminal is divided into two concourses, with the north terminal containing Concourses A and B, and the south terminal containing Concourses C and D.[\\[98\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-terminal_map-98)\n\n[![Image 12](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Dulles_Terminal_-_15.jpg/250px-Dulles_Terminal_-_15.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dulles_Terminal_-_15.jpg)\n\nThe interior of Concourses A & B.\n\nConcourses A and B are located in the midfield terminal building closer to the main terminal. They are utilized by all non-United flights as well as a limited number of [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\") flights. Concourse A has 47 gates, located in the eastern half of the north midfield terminal. It consists of a permanent ground-level set of gates designed for small planes and United Express flights, and several former Concourse B gates.[\\[99\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-99) The concourse is primarily used for international flights. [Air France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_France \"Air France\") and [KLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KLM \"KLM\") have a lounge opposite gate A19, [Etihad Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etihad_Airways \"Etihad Airways\") operates a First and Business Class lounge across from gate A15, and [Virgin Atlantic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virgin_Atlantic \"Virgin Atlantic\") has a Clubhouse lounge adjacent to gate A31. Concourse A's AeroTrain station is located between gates A6 and A14.[\\[98\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-terminal_map-98)\n\n[![Image 13](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/KLM_Boeing_787-9_PH-BHE_at_IAD_%2843463954224%29.jpg/250px-KLM_Boeing_787-9_PH-BHE_at_IAD_%2843463954224%29.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:KLM_Boeing_787-9_PH-BHE_at_IAD_/(43463954224/).jpg)\n\nA [KLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KLM \"KLM\") [Boeing 787](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_787 \"Boeing 787\") aircraft serving Terminal A in June 2018.\n\nConcourse B has 28 gates, located in the western half of the terminal. It is the first of the permanent elevated midfield concourses. Originally constructed in 1998 and designed by [Skidmore, Owings & Merrill](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skidmore,_Owings_%26_Merrill \"Skidmore, Owings & Merrill\") and [Hellmuth, Obata and Kassabaum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellmuth,_Obata_and_Kassabaum \"Hellmuth, Obata and Kassabaum\"), the B concourse contained 20 gates. In 2003, 4 additional gates were added, followed by a 15-gate expansion in 2008.[\\[100\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-100) In addition to the AeroTrain station located between gates B51 and B62, Concourse B is also connected to the main terminal by an underground walkway. Concourse B is used by some international carriers, and is also utilized by almost all non-United domestic and Canada flights. The facility also includes a [British Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Airways \"British Airways\") Galleries lounge adjacent to the AeroTrain station, a [Lufthansa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufthansa \"Lufthansa\") lounge between gates B49 and B51, and a [Turkish Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkish_Airlines \"Turkish Airlines\") lounge near gate B43.[\\[101\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-upgrd.com-101)\n\n[![Image 14](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/KIAD003.jpg/250px-KIAD003.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:KIAD003.jpg)\n\nThe interior of Concourse C and D, where [United](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\")'s hub operation is based.\n\nConcourses C and D are located in the south midfield terminal, and are used for [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\") flights, including all mainline flights and most [United Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Express \"United Express\") regional flights (save for a few that use Concourse A).\n\nThese concourses were constructed in 1983 as temporary facilities and designed by [Hellmuth, Obata and Kassabaum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellmuth,_Obata_and_Kassabaum \"Hellmuth, Obata and Kassabaum\"). The two concourses have 22 gates each, numbered C1–C28 and D1–D32, with odd-numbered gates on the north side of the building and even numbered gates on the south side. Concourse C composes the eastern half of the terminal and Concourse D composes the western half.[\\[102\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-archexp-102)[\\[103\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Httpwwwmwaacomdulleshtm-103) The C/D concourses were given a facelift in 2006 that included light-fixture upgrades, new paint finishes, new ceiling grids and tiles, heating and air conditioning replacement, and complete restroom renovations.[\\[103\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Httpwwwmwaacomdulleshtm-103)\n\n[![Image 15](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/United_Airlines_B777-200%2C_N781UA%2C_February_2011%2C_IADairport.jpg/250px-United_Airlines_B777-200%2C_N781UA%2C_February_2011%2C_IADairport.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:United_Airlines_B777-200,_N781UA,_February_2011,_IADairport.jpg)\n\nA pair of [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\") [Boeing 777-200](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_777 \"Boeing 777\") aircraft at Terminal C in February 2011.\n\nWhile all gates in Concourses C/D can be utilized for both domestic and international departures, all United international arrivals are conducted at gates C1-C14. These gates contain two exit configurations depending on the arriving flight. Domestic passengers and international passengers from airports with [U.S. customs pre-clearance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_border_preclearance \"United States border preclearance\") exit directly into the concourse, while international arrivals from airports without border pre-clearance are redirected down a sterile corridor to U.S. Customs & Immigration. Passengers arriving from international destinations who are ending their journey at Dulles are then transported by mobile lounge to the International Arrivals Building, while passengers making onward connections are directed to a separate customs facility located on the ground floor of Concourse C. After being screened by TSA at a dedicated security checkpoint within the facility, these passengers then take escalators that deposit them in Concourse C near gate C7.[\\[104\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-liveandleftsfly-104)\n\nA new and permanent C/D concourse (also called \"Tier 2\") is planned as part of the D2 Dulles Development Project. The new building is to include a three-level structure with 44 airline gates and similar amenities to Concourse B.[\\[103\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Httpwwwmwaacomdulleshtm-103) The concourse plan includes a dedicated mezzanine corridor with moving sidewalks to serve international passengers. The design and construction of the new C/D concourse has not been scheduled.[\\[103\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Httpwwwmwaacomdulleshtm-103) When built, it is planned that both terminals will be connected to the main terminal and other concourses via the AeroTrain. To that extent, the AeroTrain station at Concourse C was built at the location where the future Concourse C/D structure is proposed to be built, and is connected to the existing Concourse C via an underground walkway.[\\[37\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-D2_Project_AeroTrain-37) In April 2022, the Airport Authority published plans for a 14 gate Concourse E to be built atop the AeroTrain station with the purpose of replacing outdoor boarding areas at Concourse A. Construction is expected to cost between $500 million and $800 million and the airport is seeking $230 million grants from the Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill.[\\[105\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-105) Construction on the concourse began in August 2024, and it is expected to open in 2026.[\\[106\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-106)\n\nSince many major domestic and international airlines have a large presence at Washington Dulles, there are many airline lounges within the airport:\n\n*   [Air France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_France \"Air France\") / [KLM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KLM \"KLM\"): Air France/KLM Lounge, A Concourse across from gate A22.[\\[107\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-mwaa.com-107)\n*   [British Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Airways \"British Airways\"): BA Lounge for First class and Business class passengers (with Concorde Dining offered for First class passengers), located opposite the Concourse B Transit station.[\\[108\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-108)\n*   [Capital One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_One \"Capital One\") opened its second ever airport lounge at Dulles. It is operated by a third-party hospitality company and was opened on September 7, 2023, located in the main terminal just beyond the TSA PreCheck checkpoint.[\\[109\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-109)\n*   [Etihad Airways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etihad_Airways \"Etihad Airways\"): First and Business class lounge located adjacent to gate A14; managed by Chase and available to Chase Sapphire Reserve holders.[\\[110\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-110)\n*   [Lufthansa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufthansa \"Lufthansa\"): Senator Lounge and Business Lounge, B Concourse at gate B51.[\\[107\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-mwaa.com-107)\n*   [Turkish Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkish_Airlines \"Turkish Airlines\"): Concourse B, near gate B41.[\\[101\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-upgrd.com-101)\n*   [United Airlines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines \"United Airlines\"): Two [United Clubs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Club \"United Club\") in Concourse C (at gates C7 and C17), and one in Concourse D at gate D8.[\\[111\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-111) There is also a Polaris Lounge located directly across from gate C17.[\\[112\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-112)\n*   [Virgin Atlantic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virgin_Atlantic \"Virgin Atlantic\"): Clubhouse, Concourse A across from gate A32.[\\[113\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-113)\n\nAirlines and destinations\n-------------------------\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=18 \"Edit section: Airlines and destinations\")\\]\n\n| Airlines | Destinations | Refs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [Aer Lingus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aer_Lingus \"Aer Lingus\") | [Dublin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dublin_Airport \"Dublin Airport\") | [\\[114\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-114) |\n| [Aeroméxico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerom%C3%A9xico \"Aeroméxico\") | [Mexico City](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_City_International_Airport \"Mexico City International Airport\") | [\\[115\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-115) |\n| [Air Canada](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Canada \"Air Canada\") | **Seasonal:** [Toronto–Pearson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toronto_Pearson_International_Airport \"Toronto Pearson International Airport\")[\\[116\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-116)[\\[117\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-117) | [\\[118\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Air_Canada_Flight_Schedules-118) |\n| [Air Canada Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Canada_Express \"Air Canada Express\") | [Montréal–Trudeau](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montr%C3%A9al%E2%80%93Trudeau_International_Airport \"Montréal–Trudeau International Airport\"),[\\[119\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Du