> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dublin_Airport\" title=\"Dublin Airport\">Dublin, Ireland</a></td><td>288,475</td><td>Aer Lingus, United</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><span><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatar\" title=\"Qatar\"><img alt=\"Qatar\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Flag_of_Qatar.svg/40px-Flag_of_Qatar.svg.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"23\" height=\"9\" srcset=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Flag_of_Qatar.svg/60px-Flag_of_Qatar.svg.png 2x\" data-file-width=\"1400\" data-file-height=\"550\"></a></span></span> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamad_International_Airport\" title=\"Hamad International Airport\">Doha, Qatar</a></td><td>283,630</td><td>Qatar Airways</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><span><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\" title=\"Italy\"><img alt=\"Italy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/03/Flag_of_Italy.svg/40px-Flag_of_Italy.svg.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" srcset=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/03/Flag_of_Italy.svg/60px-Flag_of_Italy.svg.png 2x\" data-file-width=\"1500\" data-file-height=\"1000\"></a></span></span> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rome_Fiumicino_Airport\" title=\"Rome Fiumicino Airport\">Rome–Fiumicino, Italy</a></td><td>276,104</td><td>ITA Airways, United</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<table><caption>Largest airlines at IAD (October 2024 – September 2025)<sup id=\"cite_ref-IADBTS_223-1\"><a href=\"#cite_note-IADBTS-223\"><span>[</span>223<span>]</span></a></sup></caption><tbody><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Airline</th><th>Passengers</th><th>Market share</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Airlines\" title=\"United Airlines\">United Airlines</a></td><td>9,911,000</td><td>56.84%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesa_Airlines\" title=\"Mesa Airlines\">Mesa Airlines</a></td><td>1,363,000</td><td>7.81%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta_Air_Lines\" title=\"Delta Air Lines\">Delta Air Lines</a></td><td>695,000</td><td>3.99%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaska_Airlines\" title=\"Alaska Airlines\">Alaska Airlines</a></td><td>547,000</td><td>3.14%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southwest_Airlines\" title=\"Southwest Airlines\">Southwest Airlines</a></td><td>353,000</td><td>2.03%</td></tr><tr><td>–</td><td>Other</td><td>4,567,000</td><td>26.19%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<table><caption><b>Annual passenger traffic at IAD<br>1979–present</b><sup id=\"cite_ref-traffic_statistics_17-1\"><a href=\"#cite_note-traffic_statistics-17\"><span>[</span>17<span>]</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite_ref-225\"><a href=\"#cite_note-225\"><span>[</span>225<span>]</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite_ref-226\"><a href=\"#cite_note-226\"><span>[</span>226<span>]</span></a></sup></caption><tbody><tr><th>Year</th><th>Passengers</th><th>Year</th><th>Passengers</th><th>Year</th><th>Passengers</th><th>Year</th><th>Passengers</th><th>Year</th><th>Passengers</th></tr><tr><td>1979</td><td>3,525,054</td><td>1989</td><td>10,399,091</td><td>1999</td><td>19,797,329</td><td>2009</td><td>23,213,341</td><td>2019</td><td>24,817,677</td></tr><tr><td>1980</td><td>2,624,398</td><td>1990</td><td>10,438,089</td><td>2000</td><td>20,104,693</td><td>2010</td><td>23,741,603</td><td>2020</td><td>8,333,460</td></tr><tr><td>1981</td><td>2,324,585</td><td>1991</td><td>10,962,328</td><td>2001</td><td>18,002,319</td><td>2011</td><td>23,211,856</td><td>2021</td><td>15,006,955</td></tr><tr><td>1982</td><td>2,609,933</td><td>1992</td><td>11,530,829</td><td>2002</td><td>17,235,163</td><td>2012</td><td>22,561,521</td><td>2022</td><td>21,376,896</td></tr><tr><td>1983</td><td>3,019,789</td><td>1993</td><td>10,987,191</td><td>2003</td><td>16,950,381</td><td>2013</td><td>21,947,065</td><td>2023</td><td>25,135,288</td></tr><tr><td>1984</td><td>3,555,771</td><td>1994</td><td>11,690,786</td><td>2004</td><td>22,868,852</td><td>2014</td><td>21,572,233</td><td>2024</td><td><b>27,254,087</b></td></tr><tr><td>1985</td><td>5,237,277</td><td>1995</td><td>12,443,657</td><td>2005</td><td>27,052,118</td><td>2015</td><td>21,650,546</td><td>2025</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1986</td><td>9,131,895</td><td>1996</td><td>12,894,028</td><td>2006</td><td>23,020,362</td><td>2016</td><td>21,969,094</td><td>2026</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1987</td><td>10,950,211</td><td>1997</td><td>13,757,861</td><td>2007</td><td>24,737,528</td><td>2017</td><td>22,892,504</td><td>2027</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1988</td><td>9,686,637</td><td>1998</td><td>15,746,342</td><td>2008</td><td>23,876,780</td><td>2018</td><td>24,060,709</td><td>2028</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>\n\nGround transportation\n---------------------\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=25 \"Edit section: Ground transportation\")\\]\n\nWashington Dulles is accessible via the [Dulles Access Road](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_Access_Road \"Dulles Access Road\")/[Dulles Greenway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_Greenway \"Dulles Greenway\") ([State Route 267](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Route_267_/(Virginia/) \"State Route 267 (Virginia)\")) and [State Route 28](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Route_28_/(Virginia/) \"State Route 28 (Virginia)\"). The Access Road is a toll-free, limited access highway owned by the [Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority \"Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority\") (MWAA) to facilitate car access to Washington Dulles from the [Capital Beltway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstate_495_/(Capital_Beltway/) \"Interstate 495 (Capital Beltway)\") and [Interstate 66](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interstate_66 \"Interstate 66\").[\\[227\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-toll_road-227) After it opened, non-airport traffic between Washington and [Reston](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reston,_Virginia \"Reston, Virginia\") became so heavy that a parallel set of toll lanes were added on the same right-of-way to accommodate non-airport traffic (Dulles Toll Road). The airport-only lanes are both less congested and toll-free. As of November 1, 2008, MWAA assumed responsibility from the [Virginia Department of Transportation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia_Department_of_Transportation \"Virginia Department of Transportation\") both for operating the Dulles Toll Road and for the construction of the [Silver Line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Line_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Silver Line (Washington Metro)\") down its median. Route 28, which runs north–south along the eastern edge of the airport, has been upgraded to a limited access highway, with the interchanges financed through a property tax surcharge on nearby business properties. The Dulles Toll Road ([VA-267](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia_State_Route_267 \"Virginia State Route 267\")) is extended to the south of [Leesburg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leesburg,_Virginia \"Leesburg, Virginia\") as the Dulles Greenway.\n\n### Public transportation\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=27 \"Edit section: Public transportation\")\\]\n\n[![Image 36](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Dulles_Airport_Station_Mezzanine_1115.jpg/250px-Dulles_Airport_Station_Mezzanine_1115.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Dulles_Airport_Station_Mezzanine_1115.jpg)\n\nThe [Dulles Airport Station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_station \"Dulles International Airport station\") of the [Washington Metro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Metro \"Washington Metro\") is part of the system's [Silver Line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Line_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Silver Line (Washington Metro)\").\n\n[Washington Metro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Metro \"Washington Metro\") service is available to Dulles via the [station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_station \"Dulles International Airport station\") on the [Silver Line](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Line_/(Washington_Metro/) \"Silver Line (Washington Metro)\").[\\[228\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-Dulles_Metro-228) Service began operation on November 15, 2022.[\\[229\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-229)\n\n[Fairfax Connector](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfax_Connector \"Fairfax Connector\") bus routes 952 (to [Herndon station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herndon_station \"Herndon station\"), [Reston Town Center station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reston_Town_Center_station \"Reston Town Center station\"), and [Wiehle–Reston East station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiehle%E2%80%93Reston_East_station \"Wiehle–Reston East station\") via [Sunset Hills Road](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sunset_Hills_Road&action=edit&redlink=1 \"Sunset Hills Road (page does not exist)\")) and 983 (to [Innovation Center station](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innovation_Center_station \"Innovation Center station\") and [Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_F._Udvar-Hazy_Center \"Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center\")) serve Washington Dulles at the Kiss and Fly near the airport Metro station (moved from a terminal-adjacent stop in November 2025).[\\[230\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ffx-bus-952-230)[\\[231\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ffx-bus-983-231)[\\[232\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-232) Formerly, the [Metrobus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metrobus_/(Washington,_D.C./) \"Metrobus (Washington, D.C.)\") [5A route](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D.C.%E2%80%93Dulles_Line \"D.C.–Dulles Line\") served the airport.\n\n[Megabus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megabus_/(North_America/) \"Megabus (North America)\") provides service from Dulles to [Charlottesville](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlottesville,_Virginia \"Charlottesville, Virginia\") and [Blacksburg](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blacksburg,_Virginia \"Blacksburg, Virginia\").\n\nWashington Flyer has a [monopoly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly \"Monopoly\") to operate cabs from Washington Dulles Airport.[\\[233\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-233) [Uber](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uber \"Uber\") and [Lyft](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyft \"Lyft\") are popular modes of transport to and from the airport, and MWAA receives a $4 fee per trip, which is included in the quoted fare.[\\[234\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-234)\n\nAccidents and incidents\n-----------------------\n\n\\[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dulles_International_Airport&action=edit&section=28 \"Edit section: Accidents and incidents\")\\]\n\n[![Image 37](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/IAD_ATCT.jpg/250px-IAD_ATCT.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:IAD_ATCT.jpg)\n\nControl tower view of IAD in 1961\n\n*   On October 1, 1965, a [Cessna 182](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cessna_182 \"Cessna 182\") crashed on approach to Dulles Airport in a field in [Chantilly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chantilly,_Virginia \"Chantilly, Virginia\"), killing all four occupants on board.[\\[235\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-235)[\\[236\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-236)\n*   There were three deaths during a nine-day air show held at Washington Dulles in conjunction with Transpo '72 (officially called the U.S. International Transportation Exposition, a $10 million event sponsored by the U.S. Department of Transportation, and attended by over one million visitors from around the world).\n    *   On May 29, 1972, the third day of the show, the pilot of a Kite Rider (a variety of hang glider) was killed in a crash. This was to be the first of the three air deaths during the Air Show.[\\[237\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-rickpowell_1-237)[\\[238\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-238)\n    *   On June 3, 1972, a second death occurred at the Transpo '72 Air Show, during a sport plane pylon race. At 2:40 pm, during the second lap and near a turn about pylon 3, a trailing aircraft's (LOWERS R-1 N66AN) wing and propeller hit the right wing tip of a leading aircraft (CASSUTT BARTH N7017). The right wing immediately sheared off the fuselage, and the damaged aircraft crashed almost instantly, killing the 29-year-old pilot, Hugh C. Alexander. He was a professional Air Racer with over 10,200 hours.[\\[239\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-rickpowell_2-239)[\\[240\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-rickpowell_3-240)\n    *   On June 4, 1972, during the last day of the 9-day Transpo '72 Air Show, the U.S. Air Force Thunderbirds experienced their first fatal crash at an air show. Major Joe Howard flying Thunderbird 3 was killed when his F-4E-32-MC Phantom II, 66-0321, lost power during a vertical maneuver. The pilot broke out of formation just after he completed a wedge roll and was ascending at around 2,500 feet (760 m) AGL. The aircraft staggered and descended in a flat attitude with little forward speed. Although Major Howard ejected as the aircraft fell back to earth from about 1,500 feet (460 m) tail first, and descended under a good canopy, winds blew him into the fireball ascending from the blazing crash site. The parachute melted and the pilot plummeted 200 feet (61 m), sustaining fatal injuries.[\\[241\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-rickpowell_5-241)\n*   On December 1, 1974, while diverting to Washington Dulles, [TWA Flight 514](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TWA_Flight_514 \"TWA Flight 514\") crashed onto the western slope of [Mount Weather](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Weather \"Mount Weather\").[\\[242\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-shaw-242) All 85 passengers and 7 crew members were killed on impact.\n*   [Air France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_France \"Air France\") [Concorde](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concorde \"Concorde\") incidents of 1979:\n    *   On June 14, 1979, the number 5 and 6 tires on an Air France Concorde blew out during takeoff. Shrapnel thrown from the tires and rims damaged number 2 engine, punctured three fuel tanks, severed several hydraulic lines and electrical wires, in addition to tearing a large hole on the top of the wing, over the wheel well area.[\\[243\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-concorde-243)\n    *   On July 21, 1979, one month after the above tire incident, another Air France Concorde blew several of its landing gear tires during takeoff. After that second incident the \"French director general of civil aviation issued an air worthiness directive and Air France issued a Technical Information Update, each calling for revised procedures. These included required inspection of each wheel/tire for condition, pressure and temperature prior to each take-off. In addition, crews were advised that landing gear should not be raised when a wheel/tire problem is suspected.\"[\\[243\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-concorde-243)\n*   On November 15, 1979 [American Airlines Flight 444](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Airlines_Flight_444 \"American Airlines Flight 444\") diverted to Dulles Airport instead of its scheduled destination of [Washington National Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan_Washington_National_Airport \"Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport\") due to the detonation of a small bomb. The bomb detonated incompletely in the cargo hold of the aircraft and resulted in 12 passengers being treated for smoke inhalation. It was later determined this was the third bombing perpetrated by [Theodore John Kaczynski](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_John_Kaczynski \"Theodore John Kaczynski\") aka \"The Unabomber.\" Ultimately it was the involvement of the aircraft in his bombing targets that resulted in the [FBI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI \"FBI\") becoming involved with the investigation and search for the \"Unabomber.\"\\[_[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed \"Wikipedia:Citation needed\")_\\]\n*   On July 20, 1988, a Fairways Corp. [de Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Havilland_Canada_DHC-6_Twin_Otter \"De Havilland Canada DHC-6 Twin Otter\") stalled and crashed after takeoff. The sole occupant, the pilot, was killed.[\\[244\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-244)\n*   On June 18, 1994, a [Learjet 25](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learjet_25 \"Learjet 25\") operated by Mexican carrier [TAESA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TAESA \"TAESA\") crashed in trees while approaching the airport from the south. 12 people died.[\\[245\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-ntsb-245) The passengers were planning to attend the [1994 FIFA World Cup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1994_FIFA_World_Cup \"1994 FIFA World Cup\") soccer games being staged in Washington, D.C.\n*   On [September 11, 2001](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_Attacks \"September 11 Attacks\"), [American Airlines Flight 77](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Airlines_Flight_77 \"American Airlines Flight 77\") took off from Dulles Airport out of Gate D26 bound for [Los Angeles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles \"Los Angeles\"). It was deliberately crashed into [the Pentagon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pentagon \"The Pentagon\") at 9:37 am EDT by [al-Qaeda](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Qaeda \"Al-Qaeda\") terrorists, killing everyone on board. Now, an American flag flies over Gate D26.[\\[246\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-246)\\[_[failed verification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability \"Wikipedia:Verifiability\")_\\]\n*   On January 19, 2024, [Southern Airways Express](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Airways_Express \"Southern Airways Express\") Flight 246 made a hard emergency landing on the [Loudoun County Parkway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loudoun_County_Parkway \"Loudoun County Parkway\") and struck a guardrail just after takeoff from Dulles International Airport. All 7 people survived without casualties.[\\[247\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-247)\n*   On November 10, 2025, a [mobile lounge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_lounge \"Mobile lounge\")—also known as a people mover—at Washington Dulles International Airport crashed into a dock at Concourse D. The incident occurred at approximately 4:30 p.m. local time while the vehicle was transporting passengers between the terminal and the concourse. Eighteen people sustained non-life-threatening injuries and were taken to local hospitals for evaluation. Passengers evacuated the lounge using its stairs, and emergency crews responded promptly. Despite the crash, airport operations continued without disruption.[\\[248\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-248)\n\nDulles has been a popular filming location, particularly in its early years when it had relatively low traffic levels in relation to its size and its elaborate design.\n\n*   The airport featured extensively in the _[Airport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airport_/(film_series/) \"Airport (film series)\")_ film franchise – in all but the [first film](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airport_/(1970_film/) \"Airport (1970 film)\") of the series.[\\[249\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:7-249) In particular, both _[Airport 1975](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airport_1975 \"Airport 1975\")_ and _[Airport '79](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Concorde_..._Airport_%2779 \"The Concorde ... Airport '79\")_ contain scenes shot both inside and outside the main terminal building in its pre-extended state. Also shown is the mobile lounge system operating in its original form when the lounges directly docked with aircraft on the apron.[\\[249\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-:7-249) _[Airport '77](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airport_%2777 \"Airport '77\")_ contains a night-time view of the terminal with a [Boeing 747](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747 \"Boeing 747\") taking off in the foreground.[\\[250\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-250)\n*   _[Die Hard 2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Hard_2 \"Die Hard 2\")_ was set at Dulles, but in fact contains no footage actually shot at the airport.[\\[251\\]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_note-251)\n\n*   [Busiest airports in the United States by international passenger traffic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Busiest_airports_in_the_United_States_by_international_passenger_traffic \"Busiest airports in the United States by international passenger traffic\")\n*   [List of airports with triple takeoff/landing capability](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_airports_with_triple_takeoff/landing_capability \"List of airports with triple takeoff/landing capability\")\n*   [List of thin shell structures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_thin_shell_structures \"List of thin shell structures\")\n*   [Thin-shell structure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thin-shell_structure \"Thin-shell structure\")\n*   [List of tallest air traffic control towers in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_air_traffic_control_towers_in_the_United_States \"List of tallest air traffic control towers in the United States\")\n\n1.  **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_ref-1)** [\"Lancaster's Hometown Airline to Serve Washington-Dulles\"](https://www.aviationpros.com/airlines/press-release/21219190/southern-airways-lancasters-hometown-airline-to-serve-washingtondulles). _Aviation Pros_. April 19, 2021. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20210608064245/https://www.aviationpros.com/airlines/press-release/21219190/southern-airways-lancasters-hometown-airline-to-serve-washingtondulles) from the original on June 8, 2021. Retrieved June 8, 2021.\n2.  ^ [_**a**_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_ref-FAA_2-0) [_**b**_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_ref-FAA_2-1) [FAA Airport Form 5010 for IAD](https://www.gcr1.com/5010ReportRouter/default.aspx?airportID=IAD) [PDF](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portable_Document_Format \"Portable Document Format\") Effective July 10, 2025.\n3.  **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_ref-3)** [\"Dulles Air Traffic Statistics\"](http://www.mwaa.com/about/dulles-air-traffic-statistics). Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority. January 2024. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20150913222508/http://www.mwaa.com/about/dulles-air-traffic-statistics) from the original on September 13, 2015. Retrieved February 27, 2025.\n4.  **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport#cite_ref-Facts2_4-0)** [\"Facts About Washington Dulles International Air